<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Khorat Plateau</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Isan Plateau, Isan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Khorat Plateau is a broad, low plateau in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>, bounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a> river to the north and east and by the Phetchabun and Dangrek ranges. It covers most of the Isan region, drained by the Mun and Chi rivers. Once dotted with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phimai">Phimai</a>, it was long contested between Lao, Siamese and Khmer states, and its people are mostly Lao-speaking.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/khorat-plateau.jpg" alt="Khorat Plateau" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Love Krittaya · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Farm_TH.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Khorat Plateau takes its name from the short form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-ratchasima">Nakhon Ratchasima</a>, historically a barrier controlling access to the area. It averages about 200 metres in elevation and covers about 155,000 square kilometres. Saucer-shaped, it is divided by the Phu Phan Mountains into the northern Sakhon Nakhon Basin and the southern Khorat Basin, and it tilts from about 213 metres in the northwest to about 62 metres in the southeast. The Mun and Chi rivers drain it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mekong-river">Mekong</a>, which forms its northeastern boundary. The Phetchabun, Dong Phaya Yen, Sankamphaeng and Dângrêk ranges separate it from central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> and historically made access difficult.</p><p>These mountains catch much of the rain, so the southwest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoon</a> is weaker here than elsewhere: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-ratchasima">Nakhon Ratchasima</a> receives about 1,150 millimetres a year, against about 1,500 in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a>. The gap between dry and wet seasons is wide, which makes the land less suitable for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, and the part called Tung Kula Rong Hai was once exceptionally arid.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Many prehistoric sites lie on the plateau, and some bronze relics of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dong-son-culture">Dong Son culture</a> have been found. The World Heritage site of Ban Chiang, discovered in 1966, yielded evidence of bronze making from about 2000 BCE, without the weaponry often linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> elsewhere. It was abandoned about 200 CE and not resettled until the early 19th century. Non Nok Tha in Khon Kaen yielded an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> settlement dating from about 1420 to 50 BCE.</p><p>The region was once under the suzerainty of the Dvaravati Kingdom and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, and is dotted with ruins of Khmer rest houses set about 25 kilometres apart along the Khmer highways. They served as hospices and libraries and usually had a baray. After the Post-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angkor">Angkor</a> period and droughts of the 13th to 15th centuries the plateau seems to have been largely depopulated. In 1718 the first Lao muang in the interior of the plateau was founded at Suwannaphum, in present-day Roi Et Province, by an official serving King Nokasad of Champasak.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Khorat Plateau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kingdom's reach over the Khorat plateau helps explain why Lao is still widely spoken in the <b>Isan</b> region of Thailand.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-xang">Lan Xang · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Scholars once traced bronze casting to China, but finds made in <b>Isan</b>, Thailand, in the 1970s showed that casting either began first in Southeast Asia or developed there independently.</blockquote><a href="/culture/dong-son-culture">Dong Son culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom ruled the central Mekong valley and much of the <b>Khorat plateau</b>, but it was weakened by wars with Luang Prabang.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vientiane">Kingdom of Vientiane</a></li><li><blockquote>At Phimai, on the <b>Khorat plateau</b>, Prince Thepphiphit, a son of King Borommakot who had earlier been exiled to Ceylon, gathered followers and claimed authority as the legitimate heir.</blockquote><a href="/state/phimai-regime">Phimai regime</a></li></ul></section>
